    "alert_id": "EX2_TDF_AKI_ATTRIBUTION",
    "title": "AKI likely from dehydration, not TDF",
    "message": "Guidelines list TDF as a cause of acute kidney injury, and the clinician is considering permanent TDF discontinuation. However, the patient's presentation strongly suggests a non-drug cause: 5 days of severe watery diarrhoea and vomiting with two affected family members, hypotension (90/60), tachycardia, and clinical dehydration. The high urea-to-creatinine ratio (24:1) indicates prerenal AKI from volume depletion, and urinalysis shows no proteinuria or glycosuria, arguing against TDF-induced tubular injury (Fanconi syndrome). Creatinine was normal (0.9) a year ago. TDF nephrotoxicity typically develops insidiously with tubular dysfunction; an abrupt rise with clear volume depletion and a prerenal picture makes gastroenteritis the more likely cause.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Rehydrate with IV fluids and recheck creatinine after volume restoration before deciding on permanent TDF discontinuation. If creatinine normalises, continue TDF with ongoing renal monitoring.",
